Yes. I agree with all three, and I think they should be treated as one consolidation/traceability improvement, not as separate unrelated features.

1. VC rejects a consolidated requisition



Your example:

HOD A ─┐
HOD B ─┤
HOD C ─┼──→ Dean consolidates
HOD D ─┤
HOD E ─┘
↓
Provost
↓
Procurement
↓
VC
↓
REJECTED

Currently:

Dean → Rejected ✅
HOD A → still Procurement ❌
HOD B → still Procurement ❌
...

That is inconsistent because the representative requisition is the workflow vehicle for all those source requisitions.

What should happen

When the representative is rejected:

CONSOLIDATED REPRESENTATIVE
↓
REJECTED
↓
┌──────┼──────┬──────┐
↓      ↓      ↓      ↓
HOD A  HOD B  HOD C  HOD D...
↓      ↓      ↓      ↓
REJECTED

Every person who is legitimately entitled to see the source requisition should see that it is Rejected.

That includes:

Original requester/HOD

Dean who consolidated it

Provost, where within their scope

Procurement, where it is part of Procurement history

VC

Audit

Admin

But visibility does not mean everyone gets an approval action.

For example, an HOD should see:

> Rejected by VC
Comment: Market price is considered unacceptable.



They should not suddenly get a VC rejection/approval button.

This is an important distinction:

Status propagation + visibility ≠ permission to act.


