3. Consolidating a normal requisition + consolidated requisition



Yes — I agree with this as well, with one important rule.

A consolidation should be able to contain:

Normal requisition
+
Consolidated requisition
+
Normal requisition

For example:

HOD A → REQ 001
HOD B → REQ 002
Dean → Consolidated REQ 003
HOD C → REQ 004

↓  

    New Consolidation  
             REQ 005

             The new representative should preserve the entire relationship:

             REQ 005
             │
             ├── REQ 001
             ├── REQ 002
             │
             ├── REQ 003
             │   ├── REQ 010
             │   ├── REQ 011
             │   └── REQ 012
             │
             └── REQ 004

             This gives us a consolidation tree, rather than treating every consolidation as an isolated document.


             ---

             But I would NOT duplicate the items

             This is important.

             If:

             REQ 003

             already represents:

             REQ 001 + REQ 002

             and we consolidate REQ 003 with REQ 004, we shouldn't create:

             REQ 005
             ├── all items from REQ 003
             ├── all items from REQ 001
             ├── all items from REQ 002
             └── REQ 004

             because REQ 001 and 002 are already represented by REQ 003.

             Instead:

             REQ 005
             ├── REQ 003
             │    ├── REQ 001
             │    └── REQ 002
             │
             └── REQ 004

             The system can flatten the items for display/BOQ purposes, but the underlying relationship should remain hierarchical.


             ---

             The resulting consolidation model

             I recommend we move toward this:

             CONSOLIDATED REQUISITION
             │
             ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
             │              │              │
             Normal REQ     Consolidated REQ   Normal REQ
             │
             ┌────┴────┐
             │         │
             Normal    Normal

             And every node retains:

             originalInitiator
             originalInitiatorRole
             originalDepartment
             originalFaculty
             originalCollege

             while the representative additionally has:

             consolidatedBy
             consolidatedByRole
             sourceRequisitions


             ---

             One more rule I recommend

             When a consolidated requisition is rejected, we should propagate the final outcome through the entire consolidation tree.

             For example:

             Representative → REJECTED
             ↓
             Consolidated child → REJECTED
             ↓
             Source A → REJECTED
             Source B → REJECTED
             Source C → REJECTED

             And the audit trail should record the actual event once at the representative level, while the source requisitions receive a linked status update such as:

             > Rejected because the consolidated requisition KSU/REQ/2026/0020 was rejected by VC.



             That avoids pretending that the HOD individually received a VC decision when the VC actually decided on the representative.


